\description{
The Euclidean distance after the chi-square transformation: the distance
that correspondence analysis (\code{\link[=ca]{ca()}}) preserves.
}
\details{
The chi-square distance is defined as:
\deqn{\sqrt{T\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{1}{c_i}\left(\frac{X_i}{X_T} - \frac{Y_i}{Y_T}\right)^2}}

Where:
\itemize{
\item \eqn{X_i}, \eqn{Y_i} : Absolute counts of the \eqn{i}-th feature.
\item \eqn{X_T}, \eqn{Y_T} : Total counts in each sample.
\item \eqn{c_i} : The total count of the \eqn{i}-th feature over all the
samples of \code{counts} (features with \eqn{c_i = 0} are left out).
\item \eqn{T} : The total count of the table.
\item \eqn{n} : The number of features.
}

It is a metric. It depends on the whole table, through \eqn{c_i} and
\eqn{T}. It is \code{euclidean(counts, norm = 'chisq')} (see \code{\link[=normalize]{normalize()}}).
It is not \code{\link[=squared_chisq]{squared_chisq()}} or \code{\link[=psym_chisq]{psym_chisq()}}, which compare each pair
of samples on its own.

It equals vegan's \code{vegdist(x, "chisq")}, which is Euclidean distance
after \code{decostand(x, "chi.square")} (Legendre & Gallagher 2001).
Differences from vegan: an empty sample is all zeros after the
transformation, so its distances are finite (vegan gives \code{NA}).

